In article <1993Mar22.114417.6289@ncsu.edu>, mrmarcel@eos.ncsu.edu (Mike Marcelais) writes:
> In article <1993Mar22.112227.1@vxdesy.desy.de>, pawlak@vxdesy.desy.de writes:
> |> Me too. The only multiplayer extension I would agree to would be a kind of
> |> 'tournament' mode - have all the levels generated at the start and allow
> |> several players to explore exactly the same dungeon with exactly the same
> |> starting characters, to see who gets further down  (and even that wouldn't
> |> be fair, as random monsters will be different).  Could be a great way to
> |> test various playing styles too... But - don't save the score for 
> |> tournament games.
> 
> How is the random number seeded?  You could just start two games with exactly
> the same number seed.  If they played the same way, the same things would
> happen.  Of course because the #s are grabed when needed. the dungeons would
> diverge but all would start equal.
> 

They'll diverge very quickly - the random number generator is used in so
many places, that one different move can make one of the dungeons have a
shop with a WoW on level 2... and you'll never know it. Having a
'tournament' with all levels pre-generated would give all players exactly
the same set of shops/altars/throne-room-monsters/etc. Of course the game
could still generate a jackal with a WoW for one of the players, but at
least some randomness would be taken out.

BTW: this reminds me of some old hack (1.0.3?) I had on my Atari. It used
something simple (like minutes part of the current time) as a random number
seed, so there was a small finite number of initial configurations. After
some time playing I could look into my notes just after starting a new game
to find that white potion=healing and iron ring=regeneration... I even
knew, that if I attack that jackal 'frontally' I'll die, but backing one
step first will make me kill it ;-). Of course I never manged to explore
first level in exactly the same sequence of steps - so level 2 and further
was a surprise.

